#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101970 :tutorial/content "When we multiply a function by a constant, the graph either stretches out or compresses. Transforming a function in this way is called **scaling**.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below.\n\n![](images/21/21f9b06e470ca0e94605eb72c5a12d2a1f2ec56645588a960f12be81ad8252ec.png)\n\nThe following table lists four sets of points. The set of points in the left-hand column correspond to the graph of $y=f(x)$, and the set on the right-hand side corresponds to $y=2f(x)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math] & Points on [math]y=2f(x)[/math] \\\\ (1, 2) & (1, 4) \\\\ (4,-3) & (4,-6) \\\\ (0, 2) & (0, 4) \\\\ (-2, 0) & (-2, 0)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$, there is a point on the graph of $y=2f(x)$ with the same $x$-value and *double* the $y$-value.\n\nNow that we have four points on the graph of $y=2f(x)$, let's plot the full graph.\n\n![](images/26/262b6515b995f6fdcf7b959dc62d9599b71476471fbc2bde394dcebfd47cabc0.png)\n\nWe say that the graph of $y=2f(x)$ is the graph of $y=f(x)$ **vertically stretched** by a **scale factor** of $2$.\n\nWe can also say that the graph of $y=2f(x)$ is the graph of $y=f(x)$ stretched by a factor of $2$ **parallel to the $\\mathbf y$-axis.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "bea7d899-701e-53ab-9bb8-42e452f0f5c7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3284 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
